	<item>
		<title>How Does Life Unfold? A Landscape Metaphor Comes Into Its Own.</title>
		<link>https://www.quantamagazine.org/how-does-life-unfold-a-landscape-metaphor-comes-into-its-own-20261009/</link>
		<comments>https://www.quantamagazine.org/how-does-life-unfold-a-landscape-metaphor-comes-into-its-own-20261009/#respond</comments>
		<pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 14:03:07 +0000</pubDate>
    <category>Biology</category>

		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.quantamagazine.org/?p=167729</guid>
		<description>C.H. Waddington’s powerful image of embryonic stem cells specializing on a rolling landscape has captivated biologists since the 1950s. Experimental data is finally confirming, and complicating, his visionary ideas.            &lt;p&gt;The post &lt;a href="https://www.quantamagazine.org/how-does-life-unfold-</description>
				<ns2:encoded>&lt;p&gt;By the middle of the 20th century, it had begun to seem that biology was almost solved. Charles Darwin&amp;rsquo;s theory of evolution by natural selection explained how organisms change and adapt. The modern science of genetics showed how it works at the molecular scale. Genes governing the traits o</ns2:encoded>
			<ns3:commentRss>https://www.quantamagazine.org/how-does-life-unfold-a-landscape-metaphor-comes-into-its-own-20261009/feed/</ns3:commentRss>
		<ns4:comments>0</ns4:comments>
<ns5:content url="https://www.quantamagazine.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Waddington-cr.MarkBelan-Default.webp" type="image/jpg" />
	</item>
		<item>
		<title>As AI Closed In on ‘Unique Games’ Proof, Researchers Raced to Beat the Machines</title>
		<link>https://www.quantamagazine.org/as-ai-closed-in-on-unique-games-proof-researchers-raced-to-beat-the-machines-20261007/</link>
		<comments>https://www.quantamagazine.org/as-ai-closed-in-on-unique-games-proof-researchers-raced-to-beat-the-machines-20261007/#respond</comments>
		<pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 15:08:46 +0000</pubDate>
    <category>Computer Science</category>

		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.quantamagazine.org/?p=167663</guid>
		<description>In the shadow of a rumored AI proof of one of the biggest problems in their field, three computer scientists rushed to publish their own milestone result.            &lt;p&gt;The post &lt;a href="https://www.quantamagazine.org/as-ai-closed-in-on-unique-games-proof-researchers-raced-to-beat-the-machines-20261</description>
				<ns2:encoded>&lt;p&gt;On the morning of September 11, 2026, Dor Minzer received a text message from a friend asking whether he was close to settling one of the most famous open questions in theoretical computer science. Minzer, a professor at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology and a leading expert on the &amp;ldquo</ns2:encoded>
			<ns3:commentRss>https://www.quantamagazine.org/as-ai-closed-in-on-unique-games-proof-researchers-raced-to-beat-the-machines-20261007/feed/</ns3:commentRss>
		<ns4:comments>0</ns4:comments>
<ns5:content url="https://www.quantamagazine.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Unique-Games-cr.James-OBrien-Default.webp" type="image/jpg" />
	</item>
		<item>
		<title>Is AI the End of Math As We Know It?</title>
		<link>https://www.quantamagazine.org/is-ai-the-end-of-math-as-we-know-it-20261005/</link>
		<comments>https://www.quantamagazine.org/is-ai-the-end-of-math-as-we-know-it-20261005/#respond</comments>
		<pubDate>Mon, 05 Oct 2026 13:40:07 +0000</pubDate>
    <category>Mathematics</category>

		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.quantamagazine.org/?p=167602</guid>
		<description>Mathematicians are facing the sudden shift with grief, anger, and a desperate search for fresh ideas: “If we don’t adapt, there’s just no more math in 50 years.”            &lt;p&gt;The post &lt;a href="https://www.quantamagazine.org/is-ai-the-end-of-math-as-we-know-it-20261005/" target="_blank"&gt;Is AI the En</description>
				<ns2:encoded>&lt;p&gt;Throughout the summer of 2026, there were plenty of signs of what was to come. Every week, it seemed, artificial intelligence models were offering proofs of decades&amp;#x2d;old mathematical conjectures. So what happened on September 8 shouldn&amp;rsquo;t have been a complete surprise. And yet to many it</ns2:encoded>
			<ns3:commentRss>https://www.quantamagazine.org/is-ai-the-end-of-math-as-we-know-it-20261005/feed/</ns3:commentRss>
		<ns4:comments>0</ns4:comments>
<ns5:content url="https://www.quantamagazine.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Qualia-MathWoes-cr.Eduardo-Ramon-Default-1.webp" type="image/jpg" />
	</item>
		<item>
		<title>Sea Monkeys Show Scientists How To Rewrite a Rule of Turbulence</title>
		<link>https://www.quantamagazine.org/sea-monkeys-show-scientists-how-to-rewrite-a-rule-of-turbulence-20261002/</link>
		<comments>https://www.quantamagazine.org/sea-monkeys-show-scientists-how-to-rewrite-a-rule-of-turbulence-20261002/#respond</comments>
		<pubDate>Fri, 02 Oct 2026 14:45:02 +0000</pubDate>
    <category>Physics</category>

		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.quantamagazine.org/?p=167558</guid>
		<description>Scientists assumed that energy flows in only one direction in a turbulent system. What they didn’t know, until they looked closely at brine shrimp, was that a simple factor can reverse the flow.            &lt;p&gt;The post &lt;a href="https://www.quantamagazine.org/sea-monkeys-show-scientists-how-to-rewrite</description>
				<ns2:encoded>&lt;p&gt;In the 1960s and &amp;rsquo;70s, colorful comic book advertisements from the Transcience Corporation in New York City promised to mail anyone who sent cash, check, or money order a &amp;ldquo;bowlfull of happiness&amp;rdquo; &amp;mdash; in reality, a small paper envelope of freeze&amp;#x2d;dried eggs. Dropped into s</ns2:encoded>
			<ns3:commentRss>https://www.quantamagazine.org/sea-monkeys-show-scientists-how-to-rewrite-a-rule-of-turbulence-20261002/feed/</ns3:commentRss>
		<ns4:comments>0</ns4:comments>
<ns5:content url="https://www.quantamagazine.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ManipulatingTurbulence-crXinyuSi_UniversityOfPittsburgh-Default.webp" type="image/jpg" />
	</item>
		<item>
		<title>What Does the Fourth Dimension Actually Look Like?</title>
		<link>https://www.quantamagazine.org/what-does-the-fourth-dimension-actually-look-like-20261001/</link>
		<comments>https://www.quantamagazine.org/what-does-the-fourth-dimension-actually-look-like-20261001/#respond</comments>
		<pubDate>Thu, 01 Oct 2026 13:20:29 +0000</pubDate>
    <category>Mathematics</category>
		<category>Multimedia</category>

		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.quantamagazine.org/?p=167492</guid>
		<description>Maggie Miller explains why our intuition about three dimensions breaks down in four, and how she visualizes 4D spaces as a reel of three-dimensional snapshots.            &lt;p&gt;The post &lt;a href="https://www.quantamagazine.org/what-does-the-fourth-dimension-actually-look-like-20261001/" target="_blank"&gt;</description>
				<ns2:encoded>&lt;p&gt;We know all about how things exist in the three dimensions of length, width, and height. Physicists often talk about time as a fourth dimension, but what if there were a fourth spatial dimension &amp;mdash; another direction entirely? How on earth would we picture that? Mathematicians use topology to</ns2:encoded>
			<ns3:commentRss>https://www.quantamagazine.org/what-does-the-fourth-dimension-actually-look-like-20261001/feed/</ns3:commentRss>
		<ns4:comments>0</ns4:comments>
<ns5:content url="https://www.quantamagazine.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/JOW-S5-E9-cr-Chanelle-Nibbelink-Default-NO-LOGO.webp" type="image/jpg" />
	</item>
